# duplicate-table-column.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (tflw `M147c`, review `A2-11`, D633):
# a `with each` header that declares the same column name twice — `tflw check`'s TF072.
#
# **What the mistake costs, and why it was silent.** A row binds each column name once, so with
# `| id | id |` the second column wins and every cell under the first is discarded — the table runs,
# each case gets half the data it was written with, and nothing anywhere says so. That is the shape
# this arc keeps finding: not a crash, a quiet wrong answer.
#
# **Refused by the parser, not the checker**, which is where the two halves of the language draw the
# line: a name a file *does not declare* is the checker's business, and here the file declares it
# twice. `TF071`'s numeric settings sit on the same side for the same reason.
#
# **The caret is on the SECOND occurrence** — the one to rename. Pointing at the first would name
# the column that loses its cells rather than the one that took them, and a reader would rename the
# wrong half of their own table.
#
# **Not covered here:** a file-backed table with a duplicate header (`with each from "./rows.csv"`).
# The columns are not known until the file is read and `@tflw/lang` does no I/O, so that case is the
# runtime's — `tests/.checkonly/data-table-extension.tflw`'s header explains the same split for
# `TF056`.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/duplicate-table-column.tflw

with each
  | id | id |
  | "a" | "b" |
test "reads {id} from a header that declares it twice"
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
